# <vault> — how to use this knowledge base

**Personal LLM knowledge base for engineering work** — the durable memory sessions read from and write
back to, so context outlives the session that produced it. **One knowledge base across every repo**, not
one per project. It is an Obsidian vault plus a git repo, and it is usually symlinked into each project
root so a session rooted in a project can read it as in-tree paths.

**This file does not auto-load through that symlink** (measured), so a session rooted in a code project
has never read it. That is why `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` carries a pointer here, and why `pickup` exists.

The machinery that maintains this — the two skills, the roles, the tools — is **Lipika**, a separate repo,
invoked as `lipika <command>`. Nothing here is authored there and nothing there is copied here.

## The one rule

**Every document is a record or a view. Nothing is both.**

- **A record is never edited.** Dumps, `reference/` traces, `sources/`, `external/`, and every orientation
  already written. Correct one by writing a **newer document**, never by changing it — the newer one
  supersedes, and the old one stays true about the moment it describes.
- **A view is regenerated wholesale, never patched.** Each thread's current orientation, and `README.md`.
  Rewriting one from scratch is safe precisely because the records behind it are intact.
- **`architecture/` is the owner's** — the one long-lived edited view. Agents write the dated traces
  behind it and contradict it with them. They do not write it.

Everything below follows from that.

## Using it — start here

1. **Run `pickup`.** It resolves which thread you are on, reads its current orientation, audits it, and
   opens with what needs a decision. One document, not a tour of the vault.
2. **Work.**
3. **Run `context-dump`** when you learn something, and again at the end — where it also writes the next
   orientation for whoever picks this up.

Read docs as a **strong prior, not ground truth**, and **weigh the age**: a weeks-old line number earns a
check, a dump written today has not had time to drift.

## Shape

```
<vault>/
  README.md                        VIEW — the index of threads, regenerated
  architecture/<system>.md         VIEW — the owner's. Stable name, no date, as-of <sha>
  reference/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md  RECORD — a trace from source, cross-thread
  grand-plans/<name>.md            a standing want. No liveness. The owner's
  epics/<name>.md                  VIEW — a large effort happening. live · parked · finished.
                                   The owner's. CITES its threads; does not contain them
  workstreams/
    YYYY-MM-DD-<thread>/
      YYYY-MM-DD-<thread>.md       routing note, dated to match the folder
      orientation/YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM.md   newest wins
      dumps/YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM-<topic>.md immutable
      reference/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md  dated traces, thread-local
  sources/  external/  values/
```

**Three tiers, and only the middle one carries state.** A grand plan is a standing want — *not started* is
not *dead*, so it has no liveness. An **epic** is a large effort actually happening, and it is the only
tier with a status: an epic is **parked** by a decision someone made. A **workstream** is one question
being answered and has **no status field** — the date it last accrued is the whole answer, so a thread
that stopped is simply not listed as live. A second shelf concept at that tier would only disagree with
the date.

Membership is prose in the epic. There is no frontmatter field for it, deliberately.

`up:` was **retired 2026-08-25** after being measured across the whole vault: it restated the folder
path in 139 of 170 documents, named a stale form of its own thread in 15 more, and nothing read it.
**Do not re-add it, and do not give `about:` its meaning.** `about:` says only *which thread this
document concerns*, and belongs only on documents that live OUTSIDE that thread -- `sources/evals/`,
`epics/`, `external/`, `reference/`. Inside a thread the folder already says it.

**One workstream is one thread of work** — one path prefix, one agent at a time. A workstream is heavy
when it carries *two concurrent threads*, never when it is merely large: two hundred dumps on one thread
cost nothing, because orientation is one document and dumps are read on demand.

**A second concurrent thread is a new dated workstream**, and its first orientation **copies** what still
bears on it from the parent, naming the parent in `from:`. Not a pointer — an agent reading one document
does not follow a link it was not told it needed.

**A thread ends by not being listed as live.** Nothing is archived, nothing is moved, so every inbound
link stays true forever. A dated folder and its last dump's date already record the whole lifecycle.

**The date is *opened*, not *current*.** Last-touched is derivable from git and is never written down.
Folders and notes both carry it, because wikilinks resolve by basename and a second effort on the same
subject would otherwise silently resolve to the first.

## The live set

An orientation carries typed items — **GATE**, **LANDMINE**, **DEAD END**, **OPEN Q**, **ESCALATED** — in
one shape:

`[TYPE] statement — trigger → consequence → dies when <condition> · as-of YYYY-MM-DD`

- **ESCALATED is not a flavour of OPEN Q.** It means only the owner can decide it, and it is what a fresh
  session opens with.
- **Every item carries a death condition.** Writing it costs the author nothing and saves the next agent
  re-reading everything. A DEAD END is exempt: it fires forever.
- **`as-of` is when the item was last *confirmed*, not last copied.**
- **An item states itself.** "See `[[some-dump]]`" is a pointer, and a pointer is pull; link the detail
  after the statement, never instead of it.

**Three dispositions at a handoff: carried, resolved with evidence, escalated.** Every item live in the
previous orientation takes exactly one, and **carried is the default** — an item leaves only when its
death condition has fired, and every disposition states its basis. Do not select for the next agent:
length is not the failure mode. `lipika orientation-audit` catches what vanished in silence.

**Recency is a prior, not a rule.** More recent supersedes older and is trusted more, but the newest
document can be thin or wrong and you may reach back. What you may not do is read an older orientation as
a rival account of the present.

## Conventions

- **Filenames** `YYYY-MM-DD-topic.md`, and `YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM-topic.md` for dumps and orientations, where
  several a day is normal and same-date names do not sort. `architecture/` and `values/` skip the date.
- **Frontmatter** — `type` / `status` / `date` / `tags`, plus `from:` where it applies, and `about:`
  only on a document that lives outside the thread it concerns.
- **Links** — `[[wikilinks]]` inside the vault; code paths as literal text, with the repo named when
  ambiguous.
- **Voice** — terse and factual, for a first-time reader. **No agent-local codenames** — say what a thing
  *is*, not the label it got mid-session. Harder still for anything that leaves the conversation: a PR
  body, a commit message or a review reply carries the salient facts and no conversational frame.
- **Timestamp every metric** — "9 KB at YYYY-MM-DD", never "9 KB". Better, cite the reference and let a
  tool answer the number.
- **Commit here.** This is its own git repo, separate from any project's. **Never change HEAD** — the
  checkout is shared, and creating a branch moves it for every session in the tree.
- **Announce yourself** — `lipika pass-log start … --scope workstreams/<ws>` before you write, `stop` when
  you finish. One shared log, so every agent can see who else is in here.

## Who writes what

- **Any session**: reads freely; writes only through `pickup` and `context-dump`.
- **`curator`**: the index, the links that cross threads, this file, and the memory pointer. Nothing else
  writes those.
- **`scout`**: nothing. Read-only recon in a context that is discarded.
- **The owner**: `architecture/`, `epics/`, grand plans, and inventing or renaming a top-level folder.

**An owner's account of what happened outranks an agent's inference about it.** They were there; you have
artifacts. When a measurement contradicts what they told you, **say so and stop** — say what you saw, ask
which to record, and leave the record alone until they answer. The usual cause is measuring a *different
quantity* than the claim: an image in a registry is not a deployed tag, a merge time is not a rollout
time. This is not deference — silently swallowing a real contradiction is the same failure as silently
overturning one.
